===[ 'Adv': Synthetic Generation based on real data statistical properties ]===

1|> ['CTGAN'][ Advanced Thechnique: 'Real World Problems' ]
    - 'CTGAN' is a 'Conditional Table Generative Adversarial Network' is a 
      'DL MODEL' designed to 'generate synthetic/artificial tabular data'.
    -> It learns the 'statistical distrubution and relationships of an'
       'existing dataset', then 'generates' new 'artificial records' that
       resemble 'the original dataset'.
    -> The interesting thing about 'CTGAN' is that it was specifically designed 
       to address some of the difficulties of 'tranditional GANs' when working 
       with tabular data.

    # NOTE:--------------------------------------------------------------------
    # It was introduced in the research paper Modeling Tabular Data using     |
    # Conditional GAN, published at NeurIPS 2019.                             |
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------------

    """ `pip install ctgan`
      from ctgan import CTGAN

      real_data = load(...)

      # first must define the categorical columns / features
      discrete_columns [
        X1, 
        X3,
        ...
      ]

      # Initialize the CTGAN model
      model = CTGAN(epochs=300, verbose=True) 

      # Train the generator
      model.fit(real_data, discrete_columns)

      # Generate synthetic data (100 sample)
      synthetic_data = model.sample(100)

      print(synthetic_data.head())
    """ the model learns from the real data and generates 100 new records

    # INFO:[ When To use this model ]==========================================
    # 1. Genereate synthetic tabular data records (good fit)                  |
    # 2. augment training datasets (potentially useful)                       |
    # 3. Generate minority categorical sample (useful)                        |
    # -> DO NOT USE IT FOR:                                                   |
    # 1. missing imputation (require additional logic)                        |
    # 2. generate time series sequence (not designed for time series)         | 
    # 3. detect anomalies (not designed this purpose)                         |
    # ========================================================================= 

